azgor wrote:
> This is a collection of YANIs by users of the #nethack IRC channel on
> freenode.net. Please excuse any old ideas that may be present.
>
> [1]: If some living foo creature is hit with turn-undead, it should
> turn undead (ie. turn into a zombie foo). --azgor

Oy, too powerful!  Also contradicts the nature of the spell.  Also
would either require adding a huge number of new monsters or changing
what it means to the game to be a zombie monster (making it a flag, for
example).

> [2]: Rings of regeneration can have an enchantment - the higher the
> enchantment the more it regenerates per turn and the more nutrition it
> uses. Negatively enchanted rings sap your life to fill your belly
> (doubly dangerous - death by choking or by life drain.) --Grunt

I like the idea of making more rings chargable, although most rings
should be generated at +1 (assuming that has the normal effect).
However, it doesn't make sense that a ring of regeneration should choke
the player itself, and a highly-charged ring of regeneration could be
way too useful.

> [3]: Wand of inversion a la Beyond Zork: turn containers and creatures
> temporarily inside out, expelling their contents --aioli

#dump is supposed to be coming in the next version, for emptying
containers.

> [4]: If you drop the amulet of yendor on astral and one of the NPCs
> gets it, then they should try and sacrifice it to their god --scwizard

This is a very good idea!  Would probably be difficult to implement
though, and there's really no reason to have to drop the amulet on
Astral so it wouldn't come of often.  (Maybe if they installed metal
detectors outside the high altar rooms?  Do they have problems with
terrorists on the Astral Plane?)

> [5]: Death messages involving wands should include who/what zapped the
> wand. --SheeEttin

Agreed.  May be difficult to implement though with the way Nethack is
written, which is why I figured the game doesn't do it already.

> [6]: Tinning the corpse of a named creature should make the tin be
> named. --SomeoneElseYes

Er, "A tin named Orcus"?  Doesn't make sense.

> [7]: Cursed grease should never wear off and should only come off your
> hands with a blessed towel. --azgor

I don't think so.  Grease is a physical, not magical, effect, and its
greasely effects are all a natural result of its ease of removal.

> [8]: Tigers should have a slim chance of dropping tiger eye rings.
> --greqrg

Weird idea.  Kinda entertaining to think about.  I don't think so
though.

> [9]: Add a donut food item for Keystone Kops to be sometimes generated
> with --rmckay

I like the joke.  I've started thinking lately that Nethack needs more
injokes, the game's famous for them but there's really not THAT many of
'em.  I get the feeling, from reading about the history of the game
over at the Nethack wiki, that the removal of the Stooges (just before
the creation of the Dev Team in fact) marked the beginning of a time
when the game started consciously steering away from jokes.  While the
Stooges were probably going too far, Nethack wouldn't be the game it is
without its anachronisms.

> [10]: Pack animals. If you have a pet quadruped and a spare bag, you
> should be able to attach the bag to the animal and let it haul things
> for you. --azgor

An interesting idea... I like the idea of giving players a kind of
mobile stash, provided that it's not instantly accessible at all times.
 There should thus probably be instances where the animal will edge
away from the player grumpily if its tameness is low, or run away when
it loses pet status -- there are a number of entertaining possibilities
there!  "Hey, come back with my holy water!"

> [11]: Shrinking and growing akin to Alice in Wonderland. If you were
> large, or a monster was shrunken, you could squish the enemy under
> foot. I believe Alice's clothes grew and shrank with her. I don't know
> if this should be changed for nethack. Changing it would result in
> thinkgs like, "The gnome lord suddenly shrinks right out of its
> clothes. You step on the tiny naked gnome lord." --azgor

I like this in general, but I'd go further.  I think large creatures in
Nethack should take a cue from long worms and be larger than one space
in size!  (They could perhaps use the same character as the worm's tail
to fill out the extent, but would mostly be square.)  It would require
redesign of some aspects of the game, some corridors would have to be
made larger, but it would add a lot more variety to the types of
situations available in the game; players could get away from a giant
by simply slipping through a smaller door than the giant, but he could
still throw boulders of course.

> [12]: wishing for sex should summon foocubi --Botje

Wishing being merely a request for an item seems wrong somehow.  ADOM
offers a few extra wishes, and I like that idea, but taken too far it
could become a nightmare of dozens of special cases.  Sex is fairly
universal, although really a player could just wish for a cursed
figurine of the appropriate demon.

> [13]: and "boy{s,friend}" or "girl{s,friend}" foocubi of the
> appropriate gender --cjeris

Same as above.

> [14]: Bribing certain greedy monsters can tame them altogether; could
> work especially well if you're the same race. --Grunt

The same race as the monster?  I thought all the greedy monsters were
demons, you mean if the player were polymorphed?

Anyway, having Asmodius following the player around the dungeon seems
undignified somehow.  The demon princes are already surprisingly
underpowered, at least let them keep their self respect!

> [15]: killing kittens should increase alignment if chaotic --Botje

And little dogs too?  Alignment already has enough increasers, but
maybe if, in the future, its made harder to get it into the
stratisphere.

> [16]: Monster: swarm of biting flies - this would occupy multiple
> contiguous squares. Weapons other than fire brand would have no effect.
> Your best hope is to get the swarm into a corridor and zap the lot with
> fire. If you bisect a swarm with fire, the unaffected squares become
> two swarms, but if they touch, they merge into one swarm. Doesn't do
> much damage, but is impossible to kill without a source of fire or gas.
> Low level players will probably need to try to get a closed door
> between them and the swarm to have any hope.  Causes blindness if you
> are surrounded by the swarm.  --azgor

Is an interesting idea.  Thought would have to be put into what damage
they would do, and if there are other ways to kill them.

- John H.



